# The build context is the repository root, and the Dockerfile builds only
# ./cmd/n0passtemps-server and ./cmd/n0passtemps-wizard. Everything the two
# binaries need is go.mod, go.sum and the Go packages, so the rest is excluded:
# a smaller context uploads faster and, more importantly, cannot leak a local
# file into an image layer.

# Version metadata is passed in as build arguments, so the history is not
# needed. It is also the largest thing in the context and would carry every
# file ever committed into the build.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Build output from a host build. Copying it in would shadow nothing but would
# make the context large and the layer cache useless.
bin/
dist/

# Test and coverage output
coverage.out
coverage.html
*.test
*.prof

# Local secrets and state. None of these are ever tracked, but a build runs
# against the working tree, so the exclusion has to be here too: an image layer
# is readable by anyone who can pull the image. The patterns use **/ because an
# unanchored .dockerignore pattern matches the context root only.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/keyring.json
**/kek.json
**/kek.txt
**/*.kek
**/*.keyring
**/config.toml
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/data/

# Repository material with no role in the build. The .html pattern is anchored
# to the context root on purpose: the admin interface templates under
# internal/admin/ui are .html files compiled into the binary through embed.FS
# and must stay in the context.
/*.html
.github/
docs/
examples/
deploy/
Makefile
README.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
.golangci.yml
.gitleaks.toml
.dockerignore

# Editors
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
